      "content": "I apologize for the interruption earlier; I was just trying to explain how the bitumen, like a very stubborn goblin trying to fit into a tiny hole, refuses to flow properly when it's too thick, leaving air pockets and weak spots.\n\nTo finish that point on **bitumen viscosity**: When the bitumen is too viscous (thick), it cannot adequately coat the aggregate particles or penetrate the voids within the stone skeleton. This creates a \"dry\" surface layer where the binder doesn't chemically bond with the aggregate. Furthermore, if the viscosity is too high for the temperature of the subgrade, the asphalt mixture fails to achieve a proper mechanical interlock with the underlying soil. This leaves a weak plane of separation. When thermal contraction occurs, the pavement layers pull away from each other along this weak plane rather than flexing together, causing the longitudinal cracks you are observing.\n\nRegarding **subgrade compaction**: This is often the silent partner in these failures. If the subgrade (the soil beneath the asphalt) was not compacted to the required density, it will settle unevenly over time. This settlement creates a \"soft spot\" or a void directly beneath the pavement.\n\nThe relationship between these factors is critical",
